+# Spec Delta
+
+## Purpose
+
+Ensures the graphhelm CLI crate's rustdoc attributes every doc block to the very item it
+documents, so an operator or developer reading any execution or MCP-tool helper sees the
+documentation of that helper — with that helper's own arguments, fields, and failure modes —
+never the documentation of a neighbouring function.
+
+## ADDED Requirements
+
+### Requirement: Execution scope helpers carry their own documentation
+
+The crate's rendered documentation SHALL attach the "one scope" block — the ONE scope the
+`execution` verbs can address for a given execution id, with an `# Errors` section refusing an
+invalid identifier — to the helper resolving the execution-addressed scope, and the "same
+addressing rule" block — repository-level records sharing the workspace/project literals, no
+execution id — to the helper building that scope.
+
+#### Scenario: Rustdoc renders the execution scope helpers
+- **WHEN** the CLI crate's documentation is built with private items included
+- **THEN** the item page for the execution-addressed scope helper contains the "ONE scope" statement and its `# Errors` section
+- **AND** the item page for the repository-scope helper contains the "same addressing rule" statement and contains no `# Errors` section
+
+#### Scenario: Source keeps each block adjacent to its own item
+- **WHEN** `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` is read as text
+- **THEN** every line between the first line of the "ONE scope" block and the `addressable_scope` signature is a `///` doc line
+- **AND** every line between the first line of the "same addressing rule" block and the `repository_scope` signature is a `///` doc line
+
+### Requirement: MCP tool-schema builders carry their own documentation
+
+The crate's rendered documentation SHALL attach the closed-schema block — the block explaining
+that `fixture` and `route` are both optional and refused as a pair, with the "exactly one door"
+rule lived on the server side — to the MCP `synthesize` tool-schema builder, and SHALL NOT
+present that block on the MCP `assign` tool-schema builder.
+
+#### Scenario: Rustdoc renders the MCP schema builders
+- **WHEN** the CLI crate's documentation is built with private items included
+- **THEN** the item page for the `synthesize` schema builder contains the closed-schema statement naming `fixture` and `route`
+- **AND** the item page for the `assign` schema builder does not contain that statement
+
+#### Scenario: Source keeps the block adjacent to its own item
+- **WHEN** `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs` is read as text
+- **THEN** every line between the first line of the closed-schema block and the `synthesize_schema` signature is a `///` doc line
+- **AND** no `///` doc line immediately precedes the `assign_schema` signature
+
+### Requirement: Reattachment leaves behaviour byte-identical
+
+Reattaching the two displaced blocks SHALL leave the CLI's runtime, wire, and printed-output
+behaviour unchanged: the two-file diff SHALL consist solely of the displaced `///` lines being
+removed from one position and re-added, unmodified, at their original position above their own
+functions in the same files.
+
+#### Scenario: Diff contains only the moved doc lines
+- **WHEN** the change's diff is computed against the pre-change tree
+- **THEN** in each affected file every removed line is a `///` doc line and every added line is one of those same `///` lines, with no other file, code token, or formatting change
+- **AND** `cargo +1.97.1 fmt --all -- --check` still passes
